templateSolve -- polynomial system solver using elimination templates
*********************************************************************

  * Usage: 
        sols = templateSolve(et)
        sols = templateSolve(J)
        sols = templateSolve(a, J)
  * Inputs:
      * a, a *note ring element: (Macaulay2Doc)RingElement,, the action
        polynomial defining a multiplication matrix
      * J, an *note ideal: (Macaulay2Doc)Ideal,, a zero-dimensional ideal
      * et, an instance of the type *note EliminationTemplate:
        EliminationTemplate,, the elimination template for this problem
  * *note Optional inputs: (Macaulay2Doc)using functions with optional inputs,:
      * MonomialOrder (missing documentation) => a *note thing:
        (Macaulay2Doc)Thing,, default value null, the monomial order used on
        the ambient ring
      * Strategy (missing documentation) => a *note thing:
        (Macaulay2Doc)Thing,, default value null, null (default), "Larsson", or
        "Greedy"
      * AdjustParams (missing documentation) => a *note Boolean value:
        (Macaulay2Doc)Boolean,, default value true, true (default) runs the
        full Greedy commit; false stops at the particular solution α := 0
        ("MatrixHi mode")
  * Outputs:
      * sols, a *note list: (Macaulay2Doc)List,, a list of solutions; each
        solution is itself a list of complex numbers, one per ring variable of
        the ambient ring, in declaration order

Description
===========

In the example below, the ideal $J$ defines a zero-dimensional variety with
four points. This method finds numerical approximations to these four points by
solving an eigenvalue problem, much like the package *note EigenSolver:
(EigenSolver)Top,. The main advantage of elimination templates is that the
internal template matrix may be reused for problems of a "similar structure"
(see *note copyTemplate: copyTemplate,).

+------------------------------------------------------------+
|i1 : R = QQ[x,y]                                            |
|                                                            |
|o1 = R                                                      |
|                                                            |
|o1 : PolynomialRing                                         |
+------------------------------------------------------------+
|i2 : J = ideal(x^2+y^2-1,x^2+x*y+y^2-1)                     |
|                                                            |
|             2    2       2          2                      |
|o2 = ideal (x  + y  - 1, x  + x*y + y  - 1)                 |
|                                                            |
|o2 : Ideal of R                                             |
+------------------------------------------------------------+
|i3 : actVar = x + 2*y                                       |
|                                                            |
|o3 = x + 2y                                                 |
|                                                            |
|o3 : R                                                      |
+------------------------------------------------------------+
|i4 : templateSolve(actVar, J)                               |
|                                                            |
|o4 = {{1, 0}, {-1, 0}, {7.85046e-17, 1}, {-4.57558e-16, -1}}|
|                                                            |
|o4 : List                                                   |
+------------------------------------------------------------+

Ways to use templateSolve:
==========================

  * "templateSolve(EliminationTemplate)"
  * "templateSolve(Ideal)"
  * "templateSolve(RingElement,Ideal)"

For the programmer
==================

The object *note templateSolve: templateSolve, is a *note method function with
options: (Macaulay2Doc)MethodFunctionWithOptions,.

